<section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">ANTICIPATE ORIGINAL RESEARCH / THE MODEL ROOM</div>
<h1>Tyrese Haliburton: A Career-Ending Injury? Or a <em>Career-Defining One?</em></h1>
<p class="dek">A torn Achilles can change everything. But how much does it really change the player who comes back?</p>
<div class="meta"><span>NBA ANALYTICS</span><span>2026–27 SEASON</span><span>24 HISTORICAL CASES</span><span>OCTOBER 2026</span></div>
<figure class="hero-image"><img src="https://images.assettype.com/tribune/2025-06-23/dosvozbv/0-02-06-0c75b444bcd99a2496a22a001bbb3212dd96418968c09ed0ad3ec66215837d6c2724db3388f0a04c.jpg?w=1200&amp;auto=format" alt="Tyrese Haliburton at the 2025 NBA Finals" fetchpriority="high"/>
<figcaption class="caption">Tyrese Haliburton during the 2025 NBA Finals. Image hosted by <a href="https://www.tribuneindia.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Tribune</a> / Assettype. Photographer and reuse rights are not established by this credit; confirm permission before public commercial publication.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section class="content"><article class="prose">
<p class="opening">What if the Achilles heel wasn't the crippling weakness <em>the Iliad</em> made it out to be?</p>
<p>In Greek mythology, Achilles was nearly invincible, brought down by the vulnerability his legend could not erase. In basketball, a torn Achilles tendon can feel like the same dividing line: the career before the injury, and the uncertain one that follows.</p>
<p>Tyrese Haliburton crossed that line in Game 7 of the 2025 NBA Finals, when he tore his right Achilles tendon. Indiana lost its floor general for the 2025–26 season. The moment was devastating not only because of what it ended, but because of what it made impossible to know.</p>
<p>Haliburton has never relied on overwhelming athleticism. He wins with timing, passing angles and an unusual feel for the game. That makes his comeback especially interesting. How much of that can an Achilles injury actually take away?</p>
<h2>Before the injury</h2>
<p>Haliburton averaged 18.6 points and 9.2 assists in the 2024–25 regular season, shooting 38.8% from three. Those are not merely scoring numbers. They describe a guard who makes other players better, manipulates defenses and turns narrow windows into open shots.</p>
<div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>18.6</strong><span>POINTS PER GAME<br/>2024–25</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>9.2</strong><span>ASSISTS PER GAME<br/>2024–25</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>38.8%</strong><span>THREE-POINT SHOOTING<br/>2024–25</span></div></div>
<p>But per-game production can fall even when a player's ability per minute remains relatively steady. Return seasons often involve changing minutes, workloads and roles. To compare careers on more equal footing, this investigation uses points, assists and rebounds <strong>per 36 minutes</strong>. That still cannot capture everything, but it removes one major source of confusion.</p>
<h2>What can NBA history tell us?</h2>
<p>The first names people bring up are usually Kevin Durant and Kobe Bryant. Durant returned looking like himself. Kobe's comeback was much harder. Both stories are real, but neither one tells us what will happen to Haliburton. That's why I wanted to look beyond the famous examples.</p>
<p>Hali Predict examines <strong>24 historical NBA cases</strong> and records each player's age, position, size, recovery period, and pre- and post-injury production. This is a deliberately small sample, not a definitive medical study. It is enough to ask what patterns appear, but not enough to pretend the future is settled.</p>
<div class="chart"><h3>01 / Before and after the Achilles</h3><small>Historical sample means · production per 36 minutes · n = 24</small>
{statPairs.map(s=><div class="bar-row"><label>{s.label}</label><div class="bar-pair"><div class="bar before" style={`width:${s.before/22*100}%`}>{s.before.toFixed(1)}</div><div class="bar after" style={`width:${s.after/22*100}%`}>{s.after.toFixed(1)}</div></div></div>)}
<div class="legend"><span><i class="swatch" style="background:#85897b"></i>Before injury</span><span><i class="swatch" style="background:#d4ff45"></i>After injury</span></div>
<p class="chart-note">Source: <a href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/Hali-Predict/blob/main/achilles_dataset.csv">Hali Predict historical dataset</a>. These are unweighted averages across players, not season-level league averages.</p></div>
<p>Across the sample, scoring drops from about <strong>19.9 to 17.2 points per 36</strong> , a decrease of roughly 2.7. Assists are nearly unchanged on average (3.9 before and after), while rebounds remain around 6.8. That doesn't mean every playmaker returns unchanged. It means the scoring decline is more visible in this particular sample than the change in assists or rebounds.</p>
<h2>Looking for patterns in the numbers</h2>
<p>Age is an obvious suspect. Older players have fewer seasons left to recover, adapt and rebuild their roles. But does this dataset show a clear age penalty?</p>
<div class="chart"><h3>02 / Does age predict a scoring decline?</h3><small>Age at injury versus post-minus-pre points per 36 · each dot represents one player</small>
<svg class="scatter" viewBox="0 0 690 300" role="img" aria-label="Scatter plot of 24 NBA players: age at injury against change in points per 36 after Achilles injury. The relationship is weak.">
{[-10,-5,0,5].map(t=><g><line x1="58" y1={245-(t+11)/16*190} x2="625" y2={245-(t+11)/16*190} stroke={t===0?"#d4ff45":"#45483e"} stroke-dasharray={t===0?"5 5":"2 6"}/><text x="48" y={249-(t+11)/16*190} text-anchor="end" fill="#b8b9af" font-size="12">{t>0?"+":""}{t}</text></g>)}
{[20,25,30,35].map(t=><g><text x={58+(t-20)/18*565} y="270" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b8b9af" font-size="12">{t}</text></g>)}
{plot.map(p=><circle cx={p.x} cy={p.y} r="5.5" fill="#d4ff45" opacity=".85"><title>{p.name}: age {p.age}, {p.change>0?"+":""}{p.change} points/36</title></circle>)}
<text x="340" y="295" text-anchor="middle" fill="#d4d5ca" font-size="13">Age at injury</text><text x="13" y="145" transform="rotate(-90 13 145)" text-anchor="middle" fill="#d4d5ca" font-size="12">Change in points / 36</text>
</svg><p class="chart-note">Pearson correlation between age and scoring change: <strong>r ≈ −0.11</strong>. That is a very weak negative relationship in this sample, not proof that age has no effect. Hover over dots for player names.</p></div>
<p>I expected age to tell a clearer story. It didn't. Some younger players struggled, and some older players came back strong. Being 25 gives Haliburton time, but the numbers don't give us permission to call his recovery a sure thing.</p>
<h2>Building Hali Predict</h2>
<p>To move beyond anecdotes, I built <a href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/Hali-Predict">Hali Predict</a>, a Python model designed to estimate changes in production after an Achilles tear. It uses <strong>ridge regression</strong>, a form of linear regression that discourages extreme coefficients , particularly useful when the dataset is small.</p>
<p>The model uses age, height, weight, position, months of recovery, and pre-injury points, assists and rebounds per 36 minutes. Numeric inputs are standardized; position is one-hot encoded. Rather than predicting post-injury production from scratch, the model estimates the <strong>change from each player's baseline</strong>, then adds that change back to their pre-injury numbers.</p>
<p>To test it, the model uses <strong>leave-one-out cross-validation</strong>: it trains on 23 players, predicts the 24th, and repeats until every historical player has been held out once. That gives a more meaningful error estimate than judging a model on the same observations used to fit it.</p>
<div class="method"><strong>MODEL VALIDATION / MEAN ABSOLUTE ERROR</strong><p>Points: <strong>2.70</strong> per 36 · Assists: <strong>0.94</strong> per 36 · Rebounds: <strong>1.37</strong> per 36. These are average absolute errors on held-out historical cases, not guarantees for Haliburton.</p></div>
<h2>What does the model project?</h2>
<p>Using Haliburton's specified pre-injury baseline of 25.3 points, 10.2 assists and 3.9 rebounds per 36, together with a 14-month recovery assumption, the model produces the following central estimates:</p>
<div class="forecast"><div class="stat"><strong>23.4</strong><span>PROJECTED POINTS / 36<br/>Illustrative range: 19.0–27.8</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>10.1</strong><span>PROJECTED ASSISTS / 36<br/>Illustrative range: 8.8–11.4</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>4.4</strong><span>PROJECTED REBOUNDS / 36<br/>Illustrative range: 2.2–6.6</span></div></div>
<p>Here's the part I find most encouraging: the model barely moves his assist numbers. It expects a dip in scoring, but not the kind of collapse people sometimes imagine when they hear 'torn Achilles.' For someone who makes his biggest impact by creating shots for everyone else, that's worth paying attention to.</p>
<p>These are <strong>per-36-minute rates, not per-game forecasts</strong>. They cannot tell us how many games he will play, how many minutes he will be allowed, or how quickly he will regain his rhythm.</p>
<h2>What the model cannot tell us</h2>
<p>The dataset is small and spans very different NBA eras, medical environments and player roles. The return period and choice of comparison seasons matter. Per-36 production can also hide changes in shooting efficiency, defensive mobility, availability and total workload.</p>
<p>The displayed ranges are calculated using the 80th percentile of absolute errors in the leave-one-out tests. They are <strong>illustrative error bands</strong>, not formally calibrated 80% prediction intervals. The model is a structured way to learn from history, not a medical prognosis or a promise about one athlete.</p>
<p>That's the point of making a prediction in the first place. Not to claim we know the future, but to put an actual number on our expectations and be willing to check it later.</p>
<h2>The Achilles heel</h2>
<p>Achilles' weakness became the part of his story everyone remembers. For injured athletes, the temptation is similar: reduce everything that came before, and everything that might come next, to one moment.</p>
<p>But history offers a more complicated picture. Some players lost much of what made them great. Others adapted. A few returned and reminded everyone why they were exceptional in the first place.</p>
<p>Haliburton's injury may define the next chapter of his career. It does not have to dictate its ending. The evidence suggests that meaningful production , particularly his playmaking , remains plausible. Whether the real player matches the numbers is something only time, and basketball, can answer.</p>
</article>
